{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-334867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have ${e}^{x}≈1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-334867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a77a02899114e11f4cbd602681801cbe4c7c18620366c8753387bf5187d3966"} {"question" "q-334867" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-334867" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-334867" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a77a02899114e11f4cbd602681801cbe4c7c18620366c8753387bf5187d3966"} {"question" "q-335020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a77a02899114e11f4cbd602681801cbe4c7c18620366c8753387bf5187d3966"} {"question" "q-335048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5,$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,4{x}^{2}=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,-x=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,5=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-1}}  \nand  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-3}}  \nand  \n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\nWe first observe that each term\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2},\\,-x,\\,5\n$$\n\nis bounded near $x=0.$ Thus, each of the lines\n\n$$\n\\text{L1}:\\,4{x}^{2}=O(1),\\,\\text{L2}:\\,-x=O(1),\\,\\text{L3}:\\,5=O(1)\n$$\n\nis valid as $x\\to 0.$\n\nNext, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From lines $\\text{L1},\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$ we know that each term in\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5\n$$\n\nis $O(1).$ Thus, by lines $\\text{L1, L2, and L3}$ and the $\\text{sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(1+1+1)=O(3)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $3$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ that\n\n$$\nf=O(1)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a77a02899114e11f4cbd602681801cbe4c7c18620366c8753387bf5187d3966"} {"question" "q-335368" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066660/state.json" "file_sha256" "de476451e29fa550ded171a5fc46c2eb212b825051bf926f91afe5bd15b258fd"} {"question" "q-335368" "field" "field-1" "attri